plan.description:
`FieldNode` (`packages/web/form/src/types/field-node.ts`) gets an optional `filter: FieldFilter`, plain data a stored `FormConfig` keeps as it is:

```ts
type FieldFilter =
  // the field's value is the operand of `field op value`; `field` is the
  // field's own name when absent; `map` turns an input's value (as a string
  // key: 'true', '24h') into the operand, and null into no condition
  | { op: Operator; field?: FieldPath; map?: Record<string, Scalar | Scalar[] | null> }
  // a [from, to] value: `field gte from` and `field lte to`, each half only when set
  | { range: [Operator, Operator]; field?: FieldPath }
  // the value names presets, each one on or off
  | { presets: Preset[] }
  // the value is a list of filters, each kept as it is
  | { rules: true };

interface Preset {
  value: string;
  label: string;
  filter: Filter;
}
```

`Operator`, `FieldPath`, `Filter` and `Scalar` come from `@bh/resource`, which `@bh/form` now depends on, for its types only here. The builder (`src/builder/build-proxy.ts`) takes `filter` on every input method and passes it through; nothing reads it yet. Type tests (`expect-type`): each shape is taken, a wrong one refused, and a `FormConfig` with `filter` keys survives `JSON.stringify` and `JSON.parse` unchanged.

subtickets.description:
`spreadFilter(nodes, value): { values: Record<string, unknown>; rest: Filter[] }` in `src/filter/spread.ts`. Each condition of the value's `all` shows in every field it matches (the user's answer on TK-75):

- an `{ op }` field with the same field and operator takes its value, through `map` reversed;
- a `{ range }` field takes either half;
- a preset is on when every condition of its filter is in the value.

A condition nothing takes, and any `any` or `not` group, goes to the `{ rules }` field. With no `rules` field, it goes to `rest`, and `FilterForm` folds it back unchanged. A value that isn't an `all` is one entry of `rest`. Tests: `foldFilter(spreadFilter(v))` equals `v` up to order, for the seven apps' configs and for a value holding a group.

plan.description:
`<Form values>` and `useStandaloneForm({ values })` (`packages/web/form/src/runtime/Form.tsx`, `use-form.ts`, `use-standalone-form.ts`). When `values` changes, compared deep so the same value handed back after a commit isn't a change, the fields take it:

- without a remount and without running `onCommit`;
- a field being typed in keeps its draft;
- `defaultValues` stays where the form started, so a reset still goes there.

A filter form's value lives in the address and changes from outside it: Back, a column header's filter, Save to view. Tests, and a section in `docs/rendering-and-submitting.doc.yaml`.

`FieldNode.readOnly?: boolean` (`packages/web/form/src/types/field-node.ts`) and `InputProps.readOnly` (`src/types/registry.ts`):

- the form hands it to the input and ignores that field's `onChange`;
- a reset leaves its value;
- a submit still sends it.

In `@bh/ui`, each binding in `src/library/forms/bindings/` passes it to its control's `readOnly`, which every control takes (`ControlProps`). A filter form's locked conditions ("Project is TIX", set by the page) are a read-only field. Tests in `@bh/form`, and one per binding family in `@bh/ui`.

`bindings/search/search-field.tsx` in `packages/web/ui/src/library/forms/`, registered in `baseInputs` (`registry.ts`) and in `ValueOfKind` as `string`, empty `''`. Its config:

- `placeholder`;
- `commit`, as `text`'s, so a filter form's search commits after a pause (`{ after: 300 }`);
- `shortcut`: the hint `SearchInput` draws, and the field binds the key too, focusing the box unless focus is in another field.

Remove the comment in `registry.ts`, and the paragraph of `packages/web/form/docs/bindings.doc.yaml`, that say search has no kind: a filter form's value keeps it. Add its row there. Tests.

`bindings/rules/` in `packages/web/ui/src/library/forms/`, registered in `baseInputs` and `ValueOfKind` as `Filter[]`, empty `[]`, its entries usually conditions. `@bh/ui` now depends on `@bh/resource`, so `@bh/form` still draws nothing (the user's answer on TK-75).

Its config:

- the stored `add: 'chips' | 'panel'`;
- `operatorInputs?: { [operator: string]: InputKind }`, the input an app's own operator's value is drawn with;
- what `FilterForm` gives it from the metadata (TK-75.19.2): `fields` (the `FieldMeta` whose `query.filter` isn't empty), `operators` (`OperatorRegistry`), `kinds` (`KindRegistry`) and `optionsFor`.

subtickets.description:
`rules-field.tsx`. One chip per condition, 28px high:

- the field's label in bold;
- the operator in the words its entry gives for the field (`operatorLabel(spec, field.type)`: a date's `lt` reads "is before", a list's `in` "has any of");
- the values as `Pill`s in their options' colors, and a ref's values by their titles from `optionsFor`;
- an icon-only × named for the rule ("Remove filter: Priority is any of High, Urgent").

× drops the condition and commits. A click on the chip opens it for editing at the operator step (TK-75.16.2). A `readOnly` field's chips are grey with a lock and no ×. Tests.

A dashed "+ Filter" button opens a `Popover` in three steps, with a back button on each.

1. A searchable list of the fields, each with its kind.
2. The operators the field allows: its kind's `operators` that its `query.filter` also lists, each in its words for the field.
3. The value, drawn by the input a form draws for that operand, through `NestedFields` with the form's registry:
   - `select` for `equals`/`notEquals` on an option or ref;
   - `multi-select` for `in`/`notIn`;
   - `text` for text;
   - `number` for numbers, and for `inLast`/`notInLast` with "days" after it;
   - `date` for dates and datetimes (a datetime from the day's first moment);
   - `segmented` Yes/No for a boolean;
   - nothing for `empty`, `notEmpty` and `isMe`;
   - `operatorInputs` for an app's own operator.

The new rule is a draft until Add, which commits the field. Cancel and Escape drop it. Tests for each step, and a keyboard test (arrows in the lists, Enter, Escape).

`InlineLayout` in `packages/web/ui/src/library/forms/layouts.tsx`, registered in `baseLayouts` as `inline` with `field: { Component: InlineFieldWrapper }` (TK-75.9). Its props:

- `overflow?: 'wrap'`, the default here;
- `narrow?: { below: number; keep: string[] }`, which `FilterForm` reads (TK-75.19.5) and the layout ignores.

The row has a 6px gap, is centered, and wraps. A `search` field grows to fill what's left, from 160px.

`InlineFieldWrapper` (`forms/components/inline-field-wrapper/`):

- the label is the control's accessible name, or a muted prefix before a control with room for none inside (TK-75.17.2);
- a problem shows as a `Tooltip` on the control, with `aria-invalid` and `aria-describedby`;
- a commit being saved shows a small spinner.

Tests, and a docs page for the wrapper.

`FormRegistry.filter?: { count: ComponentType<FilterCountProps>; sheet: ComponentType<FilterSheetProps> }` in `packages/web/form/src/types/registry.ts`, beside `root` and `action`:

```ts
interface FilterCountProps {
  shown?: number;
  total?: number;
  // the resource's name for its rows: ResourceMeta.label
  label: { one: string; other: string };
  loading: boolean;
}
```

`FilterCount` in `@bh/ui` (`forms/components/filter-count/`):

- reads "12 of 40 items", or "40 items" when all are shown, numbers in IBM Plex Mono, inside `<output aria-live="polite">`;
- "Counting" with a spinner while loading;
- the count in the danger tone at 0.

`FilterSheet` is TK-75.18.2's. Tests.

`FilterSheet` in `@bh/ui` (`forms/components/filter-sheet/`), the registry's `filter.sheet`:

```ts
interface FilterSheetProps {
  title: string;
  // the conditions the sheet's fields hold, on the button's badge
  count: number;
  open: boolean;
  onOpenChange: (open: boolean) => void;
  // the sheet's own form
  children: ReactNode;
}
```

A 44px "Filters" button with the count as its badge opens a `Drawer` from the bottom, with a grabber and the title. Its children scroll inside it and the form's buttons stick to its foot. The content is portaled, so the sheet's form isn't nested in the bar's. Focus goes back to the button on close. Tests, and a docs example at phone width.

Props: `form: FormConfig`, `value: { search?: string; filter?: Filter }`, `onChange(next: { search?: string; filter: Filter })` and `slots?: { end?: ReactNode }`. It draws `<Form>` with the nearest `FormProvider`'s registry:

- `values` are `spreadFilter(form.nodes, value)` (TK-75.4.2, TK-75.6), and the start is every field empty;
- with no submit node in the tree, each commit runs `dropShared` (TK-75.4.3), then `foldFilter`, then `onChange`;
- with a submit node, `onChange` runs on submit, and the form is dirty until then ("1 change waiting");
- the config's reset, with `to: 'start'` (TK-75.7), is Clear, which empties the form after an Apply too;
- `rest` from the spread is folded back unchanged.

Tests: applying at once; applying with a button; Clear after an Apply; a new `value` from outside (Back) updating the fields without `onChange`; a hidden field writing nothing.

New props:

- `meta: ResourceMeta`, `operators: OperatorRegistry` and `kinds: KindRegistry`;
- `optionsFor?(resource: string, text: string): Promise<Option[]>`;
- `countsBy?(field: FieldPath, value): Promise<Record<string, number>>`;
- `countFor?(value): Promise<number>`.

Each field with a `filter` takes these from the `FieldMeta` its `filter.field` (or name) names:

- its label, unless the config gives one;
- an option field's options with their colors (`OptionMeta` to `Option`, archived ones left out unless the value holds them), or a path's attribute options (`status.kind`);
- a ref's options from `optionsFor`, as an async config key so the input searches;
- a `map`'s keys as its options when the field has none.

`counts: true` in a field's config fills each option's `count` from `countsBy`, a grouped query on that field (TK-11.2.5). Each count is the rows picking that option would show: `FilterForm` passes `countsBy` the value with that field's own condition left out and every other filter as it is, so picking a second status doesn't zero the others. A preset field's counts come from `countFor` per preset, of the value with that field's condition swapped for the preset's. Both are asked again 300 ms after the value changes. The `rules` field gets `fields`, `operators`, `kinds` and `optionsFor` (TK-75.16).

Building throws, naming the field, when a `filter` names a field `meta` lacks or doesn't let be filtered, or an operator its kind or `query.filter` doesn't allow. Tests over a made-up `ResourceMeta`.

`narrowOf(form, keep)` in `src/filter/narrow.ts` splits a `FormConfig` into two:

- the bar: the kept fields and the rest of its nodes, with a slot where the first moved field was;
- the sheet: the moved fields, the config's reset, and a submit labelled `'Show {count} items'` unless the config gives one.

`FilterForm` reads the `inline` layout's `narrow: { below, keep }` (TK-75.17.1) and watches its own width with a `ResizeObserver`. Below `below`, it draws the bar, applying at once, and puts the registry's `filter.sheet` (TK-75.18.2) in that slot, its badge the number of conditions the moved fields hold.

The sheet's form:

- starts from the current value and applies through `onChange` on submit, then closes;
- fills `{count}` from `countFor(value)`, asked 300 ms after its value changes (the user's answer on TK-75);
- draws its fields with the registry's own field wrapper, labels above;
- stops its submit and reset events at the sheet, since React carries a portal's events up to the bar's form.

Tests: the kept fields apply at once, the sheet's only on submit; the count label; closing on Apply; Clear in the sheet.

