plan.description:
`POST /api/<resource>/query`. Answers rows, the next cursor, the total, and groups with counts. Every part of the body is optional: `{}` answers the first page of every row, in the resource's own sort.

```ts
/** POST /api/<resource>/query: the body */
interface Query {
  // which rows, by their fields (TK-11.2.1, TK-11.2.2)
  filter?: Filter;
  // which rows, by their text; the filter and the search both hold
  // (TK-11.2.6)
  search?: string;
  // their order; ResourceMeta.sort when absent (TK-11.2.3)
  sort?: Sort[];
  // which page: numbered, or the one after a cursor (TK-11.2.4)
  page?: Page;
  // groups and their counts instead of rows (TK-11.2.5)
  group?: GroupBy;
  // which fields a row carries (TK-11.2.6)
  fields?: string[];
}

/** POST /api/<resource>/query: the answer */
interface QueryResult {
  // [] when the query grouped
  rows: Row[];
  // when the query grouped
  groups?: Group[];
  // the next page's cursor, and the total (TK-11.2.4)
  page: PageResult;
}
```

One project's open items, most urgent first, and how many there are:

```json
{
  "filter": { "all": [
    { "field": "project", "op": "equals", "value": "TIX" },
    { "field": "status.kind", "op": "in", "value": ["todo", "active", "blocked"] }
  ] },
  "sort": [{ "field": "priority", "dir": "asc" }],
  "page": { "take": 100 }
}
```

The body is checked against the resource's metadata before it runs: an unknown field, an operator, sort or group the field doesn't allow, or a value of the wrong kind answers `invalid`, with the path to each problem (TK-11.4).

