subtickets.description:
A filter is data, never code: a view stores it as it is (TK-16), and the server turns it into SQL.

```ts
type Filter =
  // every one holds; { all: [] } keeps every row
  | { all: Filter[] }
  // at least one holds; { any: [] } keeps none
  | { any: Filter[] }
  | { not: Filter }
  | Condition;

interface Condition {
  field: FieldPath;
  // one the field allows: FieldMeta.query.filter (TK-11.2.2)
  op: Operator;
  // what `op` compares with, as its OperatorSpec's operand schema says
  // (TK-11.2.2); absent for empty and notEmpty
  value?: Scalar | Scalar[];
}

/**
 * A field's key: 'status'. Or an option field's key, a dot and one of its
 * attributes: 'status.kind'. One level, no deeper.
 */
type FieldPath = string;
```

Items assigned to user 7 or to no one, that aren't finished:

```json
{ "all": [
  { "not": { "field": "status.kind", "op": "in", "value": ["done", "dropped"] } },
  { "any": [
    { "field": "assignee", "op": "equals", "value": 7 },
    { "field": "assignee", "op": "empty" }
  ] }
] }
```

A path is how a filter reaches what an option means rather than which option it is: `status.kind in [done]` holds for every project's finished statuses, whatever each project calls them. A path filters, sorts and groups like a field of the attribute's kind.

Operators are a registry, as kinds and formats are (chosen on TK-11): bh registers twelve, and an app adds its own. Each kind allows the operators that mean something for it. A field may allow fewer (`FieldMeta.query.filter`), for instance where an operator would need an index the field doesn't have: items-core's reason for opting in per operator (`resources/utils/filter-operators.ts`).

```ts
// a registered operator's name: bh's ('in') or an app's own
type Operator = string;

/**
 * What the query knows of a registered operator, to check a condition that
 * names it (TK-11.5). An app registers its own beside bh's twelve, with its SQL
 * for each adapter it runs on (TK-12.2, TK-13.2) and the value input the
 * FilterForm shows for it (TK-75).
 */
interface OperatorSpec {
  // 'in'
  name: string;
  // how a filter reads it: 'is any of'
  label: string;
  // how it reads where `label` doesn't fit: on a kind ({ kinds: { date:
  // 'is before' } }), or on a field holding a list ({ many: 'has any of' })
  labels?: { kinds?: { [kind: string]: string }; many?: string };
  // its operand's valibot schema, given the field's value schema: `in` takes 1
  // to 100 values; none → it takes no operand, as `empty` does
  operand?: (value: v.GenericSchema) => v.GenericSchema;
  // does a row's value pass, given the operand? Plain JavaScript, the same
  // rules as each adapter's SQL for it (TK-12.2, TK-13.2.1), which lives in
  // that adapter, never here. viewer: the signed-in user's id, for an
  // operator about who is asking (isMe)
  matches: (value: Scalar | Scalar[] | null, operand?: Scalar | Scalar[], viewer?: Scalar) => boolean;
}

/**
 * bh's operators, entries of the registry like an app's own: no type or
 * table of their names beside them. Each one's operand is what it compares
 * with, on a field whose values are V (its KindValue); its matches, whether a
 * row's value passes.
 */
const baseOperators: readonly OperatorSpec[] = [
  // V
  { name: 'equals',     label: 'is',           operand: (value) => value, matches: (a, b) => a === b },
  { name: 'notEquals',  label: 'is not',       operand: (value) => value, matches: (a, b) => a !== b },
  // 1 to 100 Vs; on a many field, any of the row's values
  { name: 'in',         label: 'is any of',    labels: { many: 'has any of' },
    operand: (value) => v.pipe(v.array(value), v.minLength(1), v.maxLength(100)),
    matches: (a, b) => listOf(a).some((x) => b.includes(x)) },
  { name: 'notIn',      label: 'is none of',   labels: { many: 'has none of' },
    operand: (value) => v.pipe(v.array(value), v.minLength(1), v.maxLength(100)),
    matches: (a, b) => !listOf(a).some((x) => b.includes(x)) },
  // 1 to 200 characters; case is ignored
  { name: 'contains',   label: 'contains',     operand: () => v.pipe(v.string(), v.minLength(1), v.maxLength(200)),
    matches: (a, b) => lower(a).includes(lower(b)) },
  { name: 'startsWith', label: 'starts with',  operand: () => v.pipe(v.string(), v.minLength(1), v.maxLength(200)),
    matches: (a, b) => lower(a).startsWith(lower(b)) },
  // V: a number's less and greater, a date's before and after (ISO strings
  // order as their dates do)
  { name: 'lt',  label: 'is less than', labels: { kinds: { date: 'is before', datetime: 'is before' } },
    operand: (value) => value, matches: (a, b) => a != null && a < b },
  { name: 'lte', label: 'is at most',   labels: { kinds: { date: 'is on or before', datetime: 'is on or before' } },
    operand: (value) => value, matches: (a, b) => a != null && a <= b },
  { name: 'gt',  label: 'is more than', labels: { kinds: { date: 'is after', datetime: 'is after' } },
    operand: (value) => value, matches: (a, b) => a != null && a > b },
  { name: 'gte', label: 'is at least',  labels: { kinds: { date: 'is on or after', datetime: 'is on or after' } },
    operand: (value) => value, matches: (a, b) => a != null && a >= b },
  // a number of days back from now: 1 or more
  { name: 'inLast',    label: 'is in the last',     operand: () => v.pipe(v.number(), v.integer(), v.minValue(1)),
    matches: (a, days) => a != null && a >= daysAgo(days) },
  { name: 'notInLast', label: 'is not in the last', operand: () => v.pipe(v.number(), v.integer(), v.minValue(1)),
    matches: (a, days) => a == null || a < daysAgo(days) },
  // no operand
  { name: 'empty',      label: 'is empty',     matches: (a) => listOf(a).length === 0 },
  { name: 'notEmpty',   label: 'is not empty', matches: (a) => listOf(a).length > 0 },
  // the row's value is the person viewing it, so a saved view's "Mine" means
  // whoever opens it
  { name: 'isMe',       label: 'is me',        matches: (a, _, viewer) => viewer != null && listOf(a).includes(viewer) },
];

// a many field's values; a single value as a list of one; none as []
const listOf = (a: Scalar | Scalar[] | null) => (a == null ? [] : Array.isArray(a) ? a : [a]);
const lower = (a: Scalar | Scalar[] | null) => String(a ?? '').toLowerCase();
// the moment a number of days ago, as an ISO string (a date compares by day)
const daysAgo = (days: number) => new Date(Date.now() - days * 86_400_000).toISOString();

// A kind names the operators it allows in its own entry (KindSpec.operators,
// TK-11.1), as an app's kind does: bh's number kind, in baseKinds:
// { name: 'number', ..., operators: ['equals', 'notEquals', 'in', 'notIn',
//   'lt', 'lte', 'gt', 'gte', 'empty', 'notEmpty'] }
```

Each of bh's kinds names these in its `baseKinds` entry:

- text and url: equals, notEquals, in, notIn, contains, startsWith, empty, notEmpty.
- number: equals, notEquals, in, notIn, lt, lte, gt, gte, empty, notEmpty.
- date: equals, notEquals, lt, lte, gt, gte, inLast, notInLast, empty, notEmpty.
- datetime: lt, lte, gt, gte, inLast, notInLast, empty, notEmpty.
- boolean: equals, empty, notEmpty.
- option: equals, notEquals, in, notIn, empty, notEmpty.
- ref: equals, notEquals, in, notIn, empty, notEmpty, isMe; a ref to anything but people leaves `isMe` out (`FieldMeta.query.filter`).
- json: empty, notEmpty.

A name a kind lists that nothing registered is refused when the registry is built (TK-11.5), so a misspelling fails at start, not in a query. TK-11.1, built on bh branch `tk-11`, took `BaseOperator` and a per-kind table from an earlier draft of this ticket; this ticket replaces them with the entries.

The rules below hold for `matches` and for each adapter's SQL alike, and the adapters' tests check that they agree (TK-12.4). An adapter's SQL lives in that adapter, in a table by operator name (TK-12.2, TK-13.2.1), so `@bh/resource`, which the browser loads, holds no database code.

- On a `many` field, `in` holds when the row holds any of the values, `notIn` when it holds none of them, and `empty` when it holds none at all; `equals` and `notEquals` aren't offered.
- `notEquals` and `notIn` keep the rows that have no value, as items-core's do: "status is not done" keeps the items without a status.
- A date compares by day. A datetime compares by instant, so a month is `gte` its first moment and `lt` the next month's.
- tickets' saved filters map onto these: any-of → `in`, none-of → `notIn`, contains → `contains`, empty and not-empty → `empty` and `notEmpty`, kinds → `in` on `status.kind`.
- `inLast` and `notInLast` count days back from now, by the database's clock in SQL and the browser's in `matches`, so a saved "last 7 days" view keeps rolling (your answer on TK-75).
- A host shows an operator by its `labels` for the field's kind, or for a field holding a list, and by its `label` otherwise: a date reads "is before", a list "has any of" (your answer on TK-75).

```ts
interface Sort {
  // a field whose FieldMeta.query.sort is true, or an attribute of an option field
  field: FieldPath;
  dir: 'asc' | 'desc';
}

// its sort part (TK-11.1)
interface ResourceMeta {
  // the order of a query that names none: [{field: 'updatedAt', dir: 'desc'}]
  sort: Sort[];
}
```

How each of bh's kinds orders is the contract's rule, the same in every adapter, never an app's own:

- text and url: alphabetically, case ignored.
- number, date and datetime: by value.
- boolean: false, then true.
- option: by the option's `order`, never its label, so "by status" is the workflow's order.
- ref: by the title of the row it points at (a user's name, an item's title), as the server builds it (TK-12.2).
- json and `many` fields don't sort.
- An app's own kind says whether it sorts (`KindSpec.sort`), and brings its order to the adapter (TK-12.2).
- Rows without a value come last, both ways, as tickets' do today (`apps/web/src/utils/compare-tickets.ts`).
- The id breaks ties: the server adds `{field: roles.idField, dir: 'asc'}` last, so the order is total: the same query answers the same pages while nothing changes, by skip or by cursor (TK-11.2.4), and a cursor never skips or repeats a row.

Pages by skip and take by default: `page: {skip: 40, take: 20}` answers rows 41 to 60, so a table can jump to any page and number its pages. items-core pages this way today (up to 10 000 rows); tickets' board downloads a whole project.

A cursor is the option, for a list that grows as it scrolls. Every answer carries `page.next`, the cursor of the page after it, however that page was asked for; a query that passes it back as `page: {cursor}` starts right after the last row it got. Rows written between two pages then aren't skipped or shown twice unless their place in the order changed, and a late page costs what the first does.

```ts
/**
 * A page, in one of two shapes that never mix: numbered, by skip; or the one
 * after a cursor. The first page of a scrolling list is { take: 20 }.
 */
type Page =
  | {
      // rows to pass over first: 0 when absent
      skip?: number;
      // rows per page: 1 to 500; 100 when absent
      take?: number;
    }
  | {
      // the `next` of the page before
      cursor: string;
      // rows per page: 1 to 500; 100 when absent
      take?: number;
    };

/** Where an answered page sits: QueryResult.page. */
interface PageResult {
  // the cursor of the next page; null on the last page
  next: string | null;
  // every row the filter and search keep, on every page
  total: number;
}
```

- A page is one shape or the other, never both (chosen on TK-11): a page with `cursor` beside `skip` answers `invalid` at `page`, since the schemas check each shape strictly (TK-11.5).
- By skip and take, a row added or removed before the page between two requests shifts every later page by one, so a row can show twice or not at all; and a deep page is slow, since the database walks past every skipped row.
- A cursor is opaque: a client passes back what it got and never builds one.
- A cursor is good only for the query that made it, with the same filter, search and sort; another query's cursor answers `invalid` at `page.cursor`.
- Both page in the sort's total order (TK-11.2.3), so the same query answers the same pages while nothing changes.
- Every page carries the total, numbered or not: a table numbers its pages by it ("page 3 of 12"), and a scrolling list says how many there are. It is a second count over every matching row, on every page; at these apps' sizes (items-core pages up to 10 000 rows) that costs little.

Asked to group, the server answers the groups and their counts, and no rows. A group's rows load when it's opened, by the same query with the group's `filter` added: `filter: {all: [query.filter, group.filter]}`, without `group`. A board's columns, a list's sections and tickets' project stats (TK-15.2, grouped by `status.kind`) are all this.

```ts
interface GroupBy {
  // an option, ref or boolean field whose FieldMeta.query.group is true, or an
  // attribute: 'status.kind'
  field: FieldPath;
  // the groups in the field's sort order (TK-11.2.3); asc when absent
  dir?: 'asc' | 'desc';
  // also answer the options no row holds, with count 0: a board's empty columns
  all?: boolean;
}

interface Group {
  // the value its rows hold; null for the rows that hold none
  key: Scalar | null;
  // its rows that the filter and search keep
  count: number;
  // what picks its rows: equals the key, in [key] on a many field, empty for
  // null
  filter: Filter;
}
```

- A row of a `many` field counts in the group of each value it holds, so the counts can add up to more than the total.
- One field at a time: grouping by two (project, then status) isn't in it.
- A group's label comes from the metadata (`OptionMeta.label`) or, for a ref, from the resource its `of` names: the fields its `roles.titleField` and `roles.imageField` name.

```ts
// its search and fields part (TK-11.2)
interface Query {
  // 1 to 200 characters: keeps the rows where a field with FieldMeta.query.search
  // contains it, case ignored
  search?: string;
  // the keys each row carries; every field when absent. The id and the fields
  // its `roles` name always come, so a row can be named.
  fields?: string[];
}
```

- Search and the filter both hold.
- The term is matched whole, as items-core's search is (`%term%` over its searchable columns): "login fix" doesn't find "fix login". Words, ranking and highlighting aren't in it.
- `fields` names keys, not paths; an unknown key answers `invalid`.

plan.description:
`GET /:id`, `POST`, `PATCH /:id`, `DELETE /:id`, with an optional version check (tickets' `expectedUpdatedAt`). Bulk later.

```http
GET    /api/<resource>/:id                       → 200 Row
POST   /api/<resource>                Create     → 201 Row
PATCH  /api/<resource>/:id            Patch      → 200 Row
DELETE /api/<resource>/:id?version=…             → 204
```

```ts
/**
 * POST's body: the new row's values; only fields with FieldMeta.write.create, and
 * every required one without a default (FieldMeta.write.default).
 */
interface Create {
  values: { [key: string]: FieldValue };
}

/**
 * PATCH's body: the values that change. A key left out keeps its value; null,
 * or [] on a many field, clears it.
 */
interface Patch {
  values: { [key: string]: FieldValue };
  // the row's version as the client read it: the value of the field its
  // metadata names as the version (ResourceMeta.roles.versionField), for
  // items their updatedAt, '2026-10-14T00:53:56.753Z'. If the row has changed
  // since, the write answers 409 conflict and nothing is written; without it,
  // the last write wins
  version?: string;
}
```

- A write answers the whole row as it is after it, so a client puts it in its cache without reading it again.
- With a `version`, a row that changed since answers 409 `conflict` and nothing is written; without one, the last write wins. DELETE takes its version in the URL, as it has no body. tickets checks `expectedUpdatedAt` this way today (`lockItem`, `apps/api/src/command/item/helpers.ts`).
- An `Idempotency-Key` header (a uuid) makes a write safe to repeat: the same key answers the first answer again. tickets passes it on as the command's `commandId`.
- Values sit under `values` so that a resource can have a field called `version`.
- A write is checked like a query: a field it may not set, a required one missing or a value of the wrong kind answers `invalid`.

