plan.description:
`{error: {code, message, issues?}}`.

```ts
/** Every error's body, whatever its status. */
interface ErrorBody {
  error: {
    code: ErrorCode;
    // for people: 'item changed since you loaded it — refresh and retry'
    message: string;
    // an invalid answer's: one per problem
    issues?: Issue[];
  };
}

type ErrorCode =
  // 400: the body, a parameter or a cursor is wrong; `issues` says where
  | 'invalid'
  // 404: no such row, or no such resource
  | 'not_found'
  // 409: the version is stale, or a value that must be unique is taken
  | 'conflict'
  // 500: nothing the caller can fix; the message says no more
  | 'internal';

interface Issue {
  // where in the body: ['filter', 'all', 1, 'value']
  path: (string | number)[];
  // 'status has no option "doing"'
  message: string;
}
```

- A client goes by `code`, never by the message.
- An app may add codes (`unauthorized`, `forbidden`); a client that doesn't know one goes by the status.
- `issues` are valibot's, each path flattened to keys and indexes.
- Today tickets answers `{error: 'item not found'}`, a bare string (`apps/api/src/app.ts`); here that's `{error: {code: 'not_found', message: 'item not found'}}`.

Every type on this page is a valibot schema first, and its TypeScript type is inferred from it (`v.InferOutput`), so the two can't drift. Two layers: the shapes, the same for every resource; and the shapes checked against one resource's metadata and the registered kinds and formats.

```ts
import type * as v from 'valibot';
import type { JSONSchema7 } from 'json-schema';
import type { OpenAPIV3_1 } from 'openapi-types';

/* ── The shapes, for any resource ── */

export declare const ResourceMeta: v.GenericSchema<ResourceMeta>;
export declare const Query: v.GenericSchema<Query>;
export declare const QueryResult: v.GenericSchema<QueryResult>;
export declare const Filter: v.GenericSchema<Filter>; // recursive: v.lazy
export declare const Create: v.GenericSchema<Create>;
export declare const Patch: v.GenericSchema<Patch>;
export declare const ErrorBody: v.GenericSchema<ErrorBody>;

/* ── The registered kinds, formats and operators ── */

/** What an app runs with: bh's kinds, formats and operators, and its own. */
export interface Registry {
  kinds: readonly KindSpec[];
  formats: readonly FormatSpec[];
  operators: readonly OperatorSpec[];
}

/** bh's nine kinds (TK-11.1); its formats come with @bh/template (TK-79.4). */
export declare const baseKinds: readonly KindSpec[];

/** bh's twelve operators (TK-11.2.2). */
export declare const baseOperators: readonly OperatorSpec[];

/* ── The shapes checked against one resource ── */

/**
 * The metadata: each field's kind and format registered; its type's settings
 * and many, its write's default and its query no more than its kind allows; its format one that takes
 * its kind, with options its schema accepts.
 */
export declare function metaOf(registry: Registry): v.GenericSchema<ResourceMeta>;

/**
 * Each field exists and allows its operator, sort or group; each operator is
 * registered, and each operand passes its OperatorSpec's schema for the
 * field's kind.
 */
export declare function queryOf(
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  registry: Registry,
): v.GenericSchema<Query>;

/** Creatable fields only, and every required one without a default. */
export declare function createOf(
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  registry: Registry,
): v.GenericSchema<Create>;

/** Updatable fields only; a required one can't be cleared. */
export declare function patchOf(
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  registry: Registry,
): v.GenericSchema<Patch>;

/**
 * Each value of its field's kind. A url is absolute, with its scheme, or a
 * path from the root: one '/', never '//', which would name another host.
 */
export declare function rowOf(
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  registry: Registry,
): v.GenericSchema<Row>;

/* ── In the browser and the tests ── */

/**
 * Whether a row passes a filter, by each operator's matches (TK-11.2.2): a
 * client keeps or drops a row it just wrote from a list it holds (TK-12.3), and
 * the adapters' tests compare their SQL with it (TK-12.4).
 */
export declare function matchesFilter(
  filter: Filter,
  row: Row,
  // to read a path through an option's attributes: 'status.kind'
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  registry: Registry,
  // the signed-in user's id, for isMe
  viewer?: Scalar,
): boolean; // throws on an operator nothing registered

/* ── For the routes and the docs ── */

/** Through @valibot/to-json-schema. */
export declare function jsonSchemaOf(schema: v.GenericSchema): JSONSchema7;

/** The six routes, their bodies and answers, and ErrorBody on each. */
export declare function openApiOf(
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  basePath: string,
): OpenAPIV3_1.Document;
```

- `FieldMeta` is one shape for every kind. What differs by kind is checked through the registry: a field's `type.settings` by its kind's schema; its `type.many`, `write.default` and `query` by what the kind allows; its `format` by that format's kinds and options schema.
- `metaOf` checks the metadata as a whole too: each of `roles` names a field, `roles.imageField` one of kind `url`, and every path in a templated field's template names one of the row's other fields. A ref field's `of` must name a resource the server serves: that's checked when the server starts, once every resource's metadata is known.
- A `Registry` is checked when it is built: every operator a kind names (`KindSpec.operators`) is one of its `operators`, so a misspelt name fails at start, not in a query. bh's operator names are entries only, never a type of their own (TK-11.2.2).
- `matchesFilter` also reads a path one key into an object value (`services.name`), for a `when` inside a template's `repeat`, where the list field holds one entry (TK-79.1).
- `Filter` is recursive (`v.lazy`), and its JSON Schema has to come out as a `$ref` to itself. Check that @valibot/to-json-schema does, through its `definitions` option.
- items-core already goes from valibot to JSON Schema to OpenAPI (`packages/backend/server/src/utils/to-fastify-schema.ts`, with `@fastify/swagger`); this ports it.

Its checks fail until they're changed.

What an app writes in place of `CopyButton`:

```tsx
const { copy, copied, failed } = useCopy();
<Button variant="ghost" size="xs"
  tone={copied ? 'success' : failed ? 'danger' : 'neutral'}
  icon={copied ? 'check' : failed ? 'x' : 'copy'}
  label={copied ? 'Copied' : failed ? 'Could not copy' : 'Copy'}
  onClick={() => void copy(value)} />
```

Where the button needs words, a `Button` with words and `<Icon name=… />`; `CopyButton`'s `label`, `copiedLabel` and `failedLabel` become its three words. `resetMs` moves to `useCopy(resetMs)`; outside React, `copyText(text)` writes.

`records.items.key`, unique across every project, so a key names one item wherever it is written. Every existing item's is today's `PREFIX-number`, and new items in a numbered project get the same. In a plan project a key is set once, when the item is made, and never changes:

- an epic: the prefix, `/E` and the next epic number (`TK/E14`);
- a feature: the prefix and the next feature number in the project (`TK-80`);
- a ticket or a subticket: its parent's key and the next number under it (`TK-80.3`, `TK-11.2.1`).

The API refuses moving a plan item to another parent, since its key says where it sits: it is cancelled and written anew, as today. `GET /api/items/by-key/:key` finds one, and the MCP server and the web address items by key.

The rules `scripts/tasks-check.mjs` adds to the schema, on every write to a plan project:

- a title starts with a verb from the plan scheme's list, today's `VERBS`, which moves into the scheme's settings to be added to there;
- `approved` and `cancelled` never together, and `cancelled` never on a done item;
- a parent whose items are all cancelled is cancelled, not done;
- `waitingOn` names no item: an item is a blocker, with a why;
- an id named in a text is an item's key.

A write that breaks one is refused with 422 and the rule's message, as a status move without a transition is, whether it comes from the web, the MCP server or a script; nothing that breaks a rule is stored, so nothing is left for `pnpm tasks:check` to find. The web shows the message at the field it names. An id named in a text must already be an item's key, so a session writes the item first, or both in one call (TK-83.2's `write_feature`).

Worked out on the server from the stored values, as the viewer and the builder work it out from the files today (`approvedOf`, `cancelledOf` and `waitsOf` in `tasks/viewer.html`; `readiness` in `scripts/tasks-builder.mjs`):

- approved and cancelled, for a parent, from the items without children under it;
- waits: its blockers and its parents' blockers that aren't done, each with its why and the parent it came from; a cancelled blocker is never done;
- blocks: the items that wait on it;
- ready: approved, without children, neither done nor cancelled, not the user's, and waiting on nothing.

The export (TK-85.3), the planner's and builder's waits (TK-86) and the MCP tools read it; TK-87.1 serves it as fields a view can filter by.
